Body:
Alternative workaround for those that prefer using the config files instead:

# Step 1: Add step-fun as a custom provider in 'opencode.jsonc':
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   "provider": {
    "step-fun-code": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "StepFun-Code-Plan",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://api.stepfun.ai/step_plan/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "step-3.7-flash": {
          "name": "Step-3.7-Flash",
          "options": {
            "reasoningEffort": "high"
          },
          "variants": {
            "high": {
              "reasoningEffort": "high"
            },
            "medium": {
              "reasoningEffort": "medium"
            },
            "low": {
              "reasoningEffort": "low"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

# Step 2: Setup the provider in your 'auth.json':
```json
{
  "step-fun-code": {
    "type": "api",
    "key": "put your api key here"
}
```

Step 2 can also be performed from inside opencode:
1. Launch opencode
2. Type `/connect` -> submit -> select "Other"
3. Enter the provider name (in my case 'step-fun-code')
4. Enter your api key

Note: the provider name  'step-fun-code' can be anything, but it must be the same name in both files.

Did some digging into this on 1.15.13 (macOS, Apple Silicon) and I'm fairly confident I found the root cause. It's the **"error" notification sound being replayed from the event backlog on every connect**, not an unread/notification-dot sound.

### Root cause

The desktop app is event-sourced. On launch the Electron client connects to the local sidecar server and subscribes to the event stream, and the server replies with a **backlog** of every event since the client's last-seen sequence (`compactBacklog({ remoteEntries: entriesAfter(journal, startSequence) })`), concatenated *ahead* of the live subscription:

```js
const backlog = yield* compactBacklog({
  remoteEntries: entriesAfter(ensureJournal(storeId), startSequence),
  compactors
});
return Stream.fromArray(backlog).pipe(Stream.concat(Stream.fromSubscription(subscription)...))
```

That backlog includes historical `session.error` events, and they run through the **same** handler as live events — there's no "this is a replay, stay quiet" guard:

```js
const handleSessionError = (directory, event, time) => {
  void lookup(directory, sessionID).then((session) => {
    if (session?.parentID) return;
    if (settings.sounds.errorsEnabled()) {
      void playSoundById(settings.sounds.errors());   // default: "nope-03"
    }
    ...
```

So every session that previously ended in an error re-fires `session.error` on connect → plays the `nope` error sound. With a few dozen errored sessions in the journal (aborted runs, `APIError`, `ProviderAuthError`, etc.), you get a rapid burst of the failure sound for ~10-15s until the backlog drains. It "can't be dismissed" because clearing the red dots doesn't change the journal — the same backlog replays on the next connect (and on the iPhone web view, which is just another client doing the same backlog sync).

The same path applies to `settings.sounds.agentEnabled` (turn-complete / `staplebops-01`) and `settings.sounds.permissionsEnabled`, all of which default to `true` — errors are just the most noticeable because failed sessions accumulate.

### Suggested fix

Suppress notification **sounds** (and OS notifications) for backlog/replayed events — only play them for events arriving on the live subscription. e.g. tag backlog entries and gate the `playSoundById(...)` / `platform.notify(...)` calls in `handleSessionError` / `handleSessionIdle` / the permission handler on `!isReplay`. The events should still be appended to the notification list; they just shouldn't (re-)alert audibly.

### Workaround for anyone hitting this now

**Settings → General → Sounds → "Errors" → Off** (flips `errorsEnabled` to false). Turn off "Agent"/"Permissions" there too if you want full silence. Doesn't fix the underlying replay, but stops the noise.

This issue seems to still happen even on v1.15.13 of Opencode Desktop on Windows. Neither the read tool nor any other tool prompt for permission when trying to for example access a directory outside of the Working directory while the config for permission says it needs to ask before doing so.

Hope this gets fixed soon as currently opencode can just extract API keys and more from external directories which it should never be able to do. It can also just access other workspaces from it's current one and possibly expose/break stuff there.



